1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:04,697
Right now, somebody on your team is deciding whether that AI feature is good enough.

2
00:00:04,697 --> 00:00:05,473
Maybe it's you.

3
00:00:05,473 --> 00:00:08,422
Most of the time that decision happens one of three ways.

4
00:00:08,422 --> 00:00:11,268
Someone opens the tool, tries a few inputs, and reacts.

5
00:00:11,268 --> 00:00:15,098
Or a test suite runs against a fixed set of examples before every release.

6
00:00:15,098 --> 00:00:19,186
Or a dashboard somewhere tracks what real users are getting, after it's already

7
00:00:19,186 --> 00:00:19,444
live.

8
00:00:19,444 --> 00:00:23,532
Those are three different instruments, and they measure three different things.

9
00:00:23,532 --> 00:00:27,724
The argument you keep having with engineering about whether a feature is ready is

10
00:00:27,724 --> 00:00:31,605
usually two people trusting two different instruments and not realising it.

11
00:00:31,605 --> 00:00:35,330
By the end of this lecture you'll know which one you're using, and, more

12
00:00:35,330 --> 00:00:37,452
importantly, which disaster it can't see.

13
00:00:37,452 --> 00:00:41,643
Get that wrong and you either ship on vibes alone, or trust a number that quietly

14
00:00:41,643 --> 00:00:42,627
stopped being true.

15
00:00:43,177 --> 00:00:47,629
Here's the same three things stated precisely, because the names get used loosely.

16
00:00:47,629 --> 00:00:52,082
Vibes is a person reading outputs and reacting, right now, to whatever they happen

17
00:00:52,082 --> 00:00:52,787
to have open.

18
00:00:52,787 --> 00:00:54,688
No fixed cases, no fixed questions.

19
00:00:54,688 --> 00:00:58,869
You already met this one in Section 0, and you already know its problem: five

20
00:00:58,869 --> 00:01:00,552
people, five different numbers.

21
00:01:00,552 --> 00:01:04,353
Offline is the golden set, run against a rubric, before every release.

22
00:01:04,353 --> 00:01:08,697
It's the instrument this section teaches you to build, by hand and then in code.

23
00:01:08,697 --> 00:01:13,149
Repeatable, cheap once it exists, and it never sees anything you didn't put in it.

24
00:01:13,149 --> 00:01:14,670
Online is production itself.

25
00:01:14,670 --> 00:01:19,231
Whatever users actually send, scored or simply watched, after the feature is already

26
00:01:19,231 --> 00:01:19,502
live.

27
00:01:19,502 --> 00:01:23,792
It sees everything, including the inputs you never thought to imagine, but only

28
00:01:23,792 --> 00:01:27,267
after someone real has already been on the receiving end of one.

29
00:01:27,817 --> 00:01:30,985
Here's the promise from the first slide, paid off properly.

30
00:01:30,985 --> 00:01:32,327
Vibes misses consistency.

31
00:01:32,327 --> 00:01:36,838
You saw this exact failure in Section 0: the same summary, scored two, three, three,

32
00:01:36,838 --> 00:01:39,737
four, five, by five people who each had a real reason.

33
00:01:39,737 --> 00:01:41,348
That's not five bad reviewers.

34
00:01:41,348 --> 00:01:44,248
That's what happens when there's no shared instrument.

35
00:01:44,248 --> 00:01:47,630
Offline misses drift, which is the picture you're about to see.

36
00:01:47,630 --> 00:01:51,926
It's excellent at catching anything that looks like the fifty cases you built it

37
00:01:51,926 --> 00:01:55,201
from, and structurally unable to catch anything that doesn't.

38
00:01:55,201 --> 00:01:56,544
Online misses prevention.

39
00:01:56,544 --> 00:02:00,625
It only speaks after a real user already sent the input and felt the result.

40
00:02:00,625 --> 00:02:04,974
It's the most honest signal you have, and it is always, by construction, too late

41
00:02:04,974 --> 00:02:05,941
for that one case.

42
00:02:05,941 --> 00:02:07,552
None of the three is optional.

43
00:02:07,552 --> 00:02:12,008
Each one is the only thing standing between you and the specific disaster the other

44
00:02:12,008 --> 00:02:12,760
two can't see.

45
00:02:13,310 --> 00:02:15,415
For good reason, most teams stop here.

46
00:02:15,415 --> 00:02:18,406
On its own terms, offline eval is genuinely excellent.

47
00:02:18,406 --> 00:02:23,059
Run the same fifty cases through the same rubric before every release, and you get a

48
00:02:23,059 --> 00:02:27,546
number nobody can argue you out of: ninety-one percent, say, moving only when the

49
00:02:27,546 --> 00:02:29,042
thing you changed moves it.

50
00:02:29,042 --> 00:02:31,535
That's the column on the left, and it's real.

51
00:02:31,535 --> 00:02:35,578
Now look at the column on the right, the assumption nobody says out loud.

52
00:02:35,578 --> 00:02:37,185
A golden set is a photograph.

53
00:02:37,185 --> 00:02:41,506
It's exactly correct for the moment you took it, and it says nothing about the

54
00:02:41,506 --> 00:02:42,503
moment after that.

55
00:02:42,503 --> 00:02:44,386
Hold onto that ninety-one percent.

56
00:02:44,386 --> 00:02:47,267
You're about to watch it stop being the whole story.

57
00:02:47,817 --> 00:02:48,601
Same product.

58
00:02:48,601 --> 00:02:50,774
Same six questions from lecture 0.4.

59
00:02:50,774 --> 00:02:51,980
Two different weeks.

60
00:02:51,980 --> 00:02:56,687
The top row is your golden set, and it looks the way a healthy feature should:

61
00:02:56,687 --> 00:02:58,498
tight, mostly fours and fives.

62
00:02:58,498 --> 00:03:02,118
That's the ninety-one percent that got this feature shipped.

63
00:03:02,118 --> 00:03:06,946
The bottom row is ten live transcripts, pulled at random this week and scored on

64
00:03:06,946 --> 00:03:08,274
that identical rubric.

65
00:03:08,274 --> 00:03:13,101
Wider spread, lower average, a couple of ones, and a one on this scale means the

66
00:03:13,101 --> 00:03:15,575
agent reopens the original ticket anyway.

67
00:03:15,575 --> 00:03:18,713
Say your product added a new refund policy in April.

68
00:03:18,713 --> 00:03:23,722
Tickets like Ticket 4471 got a lot more common, and none of March's fifty cases saw

69
00:03:23,722 --> 00:03:23,963
one.

70
00:03:23,963 --> 00:03:28,730
Nothing here means the rubric is broken, or that March's fifty cases were badly

71
00:03:28,730 --> 00:03:29,153
chosen.

72
00:03:29,153 --> 00:03:32,291
It means production moved and the photograph didn't.

73
00:03:32,841 --> 00:03:36,279
Notice the word doing the work in that definition: identical.

74
00:03:36,279 --> 00:03:38,928
Drift isn't the rubric disagreeing with itself.

75
00:03:38,928 --> 00:03:43,042
It's the same six questions, asked of a world that stopped holding still.

76
00:03:43,042 --> 00:03:46,536
The exercise underneath it costs you twenty minutes a release.

77
00:03:46,536 --> 00:03:49,918
Ten transcripts, the rubric you already own, no new tooling.

78
00:03:49,918 --> 00:03:53,356
It's the cheapest early-warning system in this entire course.

79
00:03:53,356 --> 00:03:55,159
Skip it, and you still find out.

80
00:03:55,159 --> 00:03:59,837
You just find out from a support escalation, with your name already attached to the

81
00:03:59,837 --> 00:04:00,739
launch decision.

82
00:04:01,289 --> 00:04:03,535
Say the cost plainly, because it's real.

83
00:04:03,535 --> 00:04:06,119
Online eval is a smoke alarm, not a sprinkler.

84
00:04:06,119 --> 00:04:08,028
It tells you something is burning.

85
00:04:08,028 --> 00:04:12,409
It does not put the fire out, and it never once sounds before the fire starts.

86
00:04:12,409 --> 00:04:16,452
That's not a flaw you can engineer away, it's what "after launch" means.

87
00:04:16,452 --> 00:04:19,429
And on its own, a handful of weekly samples is noisy.

88
00:04:19,429 --> 00:04:24,146
Two failures out of ten live tickets: is that a true twenty percent failure rate, or

89
00:04:24,146 --> 00:04:26,842
a true five percent rate having an unlucky week?

90
00:04:26,842 --> 00:04:30,156
At that sample size, nobody in the room can tell you which.

91
00:04:30,156 --> 00:04:34,705
You'll meet the fix for that later in this section: how many samples turn a hunch

92
00:04:34,705 --> 00:04:36,333
into a number you can defend.

93
00:04:36,883 --> 00:04:40,249
Three mechanisms, and this section builds all three properly.

94
00:04:40,249 --> 00:04:42,621
You'll turn vibes into something scoreable.

95
00:04:42,621 --> 00:04:47,145
You'll build the offline instrument yourself, first where you can see every moving

96
00:04:47,145 --> 00:04:48,193
part, then in code.

97
00:04:48,193 --> 00:04:52,827
And you'll give online signal a proper home instead of a Slack message someone sends

98
00:04:52,827 --> 00:04:54,262
when a customer complains.

99
00:04:54,262 --> 00:04:57,793
Next lecture, you build the fifty cases that instrument runs on.

100
00:04:57,793 --> 00:05:01,434
Not fifty at random: fifty chosen for exactly what they teach you.

101
00:05:01,434 --> 00:05:06,013
Do all three properly, and "is it ready" stops being an argument and starts being a

102
00:05:06,013 --> 00:05:08,109
number everyone in the room can check.
